Chapter: DiagKbd
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Works chapter GopUsbKbd
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 We say:

Section: Reading The Cells

 Every cell here is written by the ORDINARY keyboard bring-up and pump
 (`GopUsbKbd`, the `kbd-cell-*` block), so this row touches no device and
 issues no transfer. It reports what the driver recorded about itself, which
 is the same shape `xhci` uses one row up.

 The counters used to live only in `uk-state`, a per-keyboard heap
 allocation, so a stage holding no `UsbKbd` could not reach them and the
 keyboard question could not be asked from the ladder at all.

  dkb-armed : Integer -> Integer
  dkb-armed (dummy) = xdiag-get kbd-cell-armed

  dkb-events : Integer -> Integer
  dkb-events (dummy) = xdiag-get kbd-cell-events

  dkb-nonzero : Integer -> Integer
  dkb-nonzero (dummy) = xdiag-get kbd-cell-nonzero

  dkb-scans : Integer -> Integer
  dkb-scans (dummy) = xdiag-get kbd-cell-scans

  dkb-bound : Boolean
  dkb-bound = xdiag-get kbd-cell-magic == kbd-cells-magic & xdiag-get kbd-cell-ok /= 0

Section: The Stage

 WHAT THIS ROW CAN REACH IN THE LADDER IS TWO STATES, AND SAYING SO IS THE
 POINT. Measured 2026-09-07 on the first run: `never-armed`, with `bound=y
 dci=3 hid-binds=2` and every counter zero. The ladder BINDS a keyboard
 (`usb-attach` does) and never PUMPS one: nothing here calls `kbd-arm`, so no
 transfer is ever queued and the four states past `never-armed` are
 UNREACHABLE from an unattended ladder run. They are reachable where a pump
 runs, which is the desk and the interactive sitting stage that asks the
 operator to press keys; that stage is not built and is registered separately.

 So in the ladder this row answers exactly one question, honestly: did a
 keyboard BIND. `no-bind` is the finding; `never-armed` is the resting state
 and is painted as a note, not a fault, because a red row on every ordinary
 boot is a row operators learn to skip. The author of this stage predicted
 `armed-silent` as the resting state and was wrong, which is why the reading
 is written here rather than the prediction (L-VACUOUS: measure that an arm
 REACHED its condition before reading its colour).

 The states past it are kept because the cells feed the desk and the sitting
 stage too, and because they are the ones the keyboard campaign earned
 (L-STATES):

   no-bind      the driver never bound an interrupt endpoint. The xhci row
                above says whether a controller came up at all.
   never-armed  bound and no transfer was ever queued. THE RESTING STATE IN
                THE LADDER, which does not pump; a finding only where a pump
                ran and armed nothing.
   armed-silent armed and the endpoint has never completed. Not reachable
                from the ladder; where a pump runs it is the silent-keyboard
                failure.
   empty        completions arrive and every report is all-zero, so the
                endpoint fires and carries nothing.
   undecoded    reports carry data and no scancode came out of hid-step,
                which is a decode gap and not a transport one.
   keys         scancodes reached the desk.

 The distinction that cost the most to learn is `armed-silent` against
 `empty`: a keyboard delivering perfectly and a keyboard delivering nothing
 both read as "no keys" from anywhere upstream, and only the completion
 count separates them.

  dkb-states : List Text = ["keys", "undecoded", "empty", "armed-silent", "never-armed", "no-bind"]

  dkb-run : DiagCtx -> DiagResult
  dkb-run (c) =
    let st = if dkb-bound == False then "no-bind"
             else if dkb-armed 0 == 0 then "never-armed"
             else if dkb-events 0 == 0 then "armed-silent"
             else if dkb-nonzero 0 == 0 then "empty"
             else if dkb-scans 0 == 0 then "undecoded"
             else "keys"
    in let colour = if st == "keys" then diag-col-good
                    else if st == "never-armed" then diag-col-note
                    else if st == "no-bind" then diag-col-warn
                    else diag-col-bad
    in let g1 = "bound=" & (if dkb-bound then "y" else "n") & " dci=" & show (xdiag-get kbd-cell-dci) & " hid-binds=" & show (xdiag-get 47)
    in let g2 = "armed=" & show (dkb-armed 0) & " events=" & show (dkb-events 0) & " nonzero=" & show (dkb-nonzero 0) & " scans=" & show (dkb-scans 0) & " last=" & diag-hex8 (xdiag-get kbd-cell-code)
    in diag-result st colour [g1, g2] [g1, g2]
